{
  "requirement": "Marks an agent request that can still accept reusable context before adding node contents.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub struct ContextPhase;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.ContextPhase` is a declaration requirement. It says `ContextPhase` marks an agent request that can still accept reusable context before node contents are added. Its contract is the public unit struct `pub struct ContextPhase;`. The text has no MUST, SHOULD or MAY clauses of its own. The behavior that `context` is only available in this phase, and that node-content calls move the request out of it, belongs to the surrounding requirements. Those are `agent_context` and the type-state paragraph in the \"Build a request\" section. This requirement does not restate it.\n\n**Test mapping.** The declaration carries the anchor `:test: crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`.\n- The test runs `cargo doc` and walks the rustdoc export tables. For each exported struct it requires an inventory declaration. A missing `ContextPhase` entry or a wrong kind or path would show up as a missing or unexpected export.\n- `documented_exports` calls `check_declaration` for each item. That function extracts this requirement's `rust,ignore` block (`pub struct ContextPhase;`), compares its signature parts with rustdoc's published declaration, and fails on differences. It also compares trait impls and indexed members. A `ContextPhase` that gained public fields, lost its unit-struct form, or gained or lost impls would fail.\n- The `public-api.md` inventory lists `struct ContextPhase`, so the export set is checked as well.\n\n**Possible gaps considered.**\n- *Phase semantics.* The phase restriction is not restated here. Tests elsewhere exercise `ask().context(...)` extensively, in `tests/validation.rs` and `src/agent/tests.rs`. The `NodePhase` doc compile_fail tests in `src/procedure.rs` check that `context` is unavailable after the node-content calls. Those belong to the other requirements, and I did not reassess them.\n- *Marking.* The test is marked through the `:test:` anchor, so there is no marking gap.\n\nThe type contract this requirement specifies is exercised and enforced by assertions, and I found no gap.",
  "references": "34070f30c190da855a0af0db8758db88cea74ad388475c8cd2f1dfaa1fb9eb00",
